      "oe:isAggregate/#resource": false,
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "an acidulous concreto salt, the crust or sediment of wine vats and casks, which when purified is denominated cream of tartar; it is white or red, according to the wine of which it is the deposit, and largely used in medicine and the arts; also a Tartar name for the dried excrements of animals moulded into cakes and sold as fuel. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 27]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:editorialNote": [
        {
          "@value": "Curated classification (group=grp_industrial_chemicals_alkalis): Named chemical (ethers, chloroform, chloral, collodion, methyl alcohol, calcium carbide, glycerine, sugar of lead, magnesia, argol = crude tartar, cordite) left in the residual bin. Layer-1 observations: 32 rows, 1800–1866. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×25; KEW_CUST 5 ×7. Units: CWTS (Hundredweights) ×32. Top routes: World → United Kingdom ×4; France → United Kingdom ×3; British Possessions in South Africa → United Kingdom ×3; Other Parts → United Kingdom ×3.",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
