      "oe:theodoridisBucket/#resource": "other_food_drink_spices",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_05_Vegetables_and_Fruit",
      "oe:isAggregate/#resource": false,
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(GER. *Kastanien*. DU. *Kastanien*, *Karstengen*. DA. and Sw. *Kastanier*. FR. *Chataignes*. IT. *Castagne*. SP. *Castanas*. PORT. *Castanhas*. RUS. *Kaschtanü*. POL. *Kasztany*. LAT. *Castanæ*,) the fruit of the chesnut-tree, which in France is used as a substitute for potatoes, and oftentimes for bread. Horse-chesnuts are the fruit of the wild chesnut-tree. Starch of very excellent quality has been lately made from horse-chesnuts by a very simple process, similar to that used in transmuting potatoes into starch. See STARCH. The wood of the chesnut-tree is the most durable of any that is grown here, next to oak, for buildings. Many of our old churches and colleges are roofed with it. [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 217]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:editorialNote": [
        {
          "@value": "Curated classification (group=grp_fruits_nuts_vegetables): Named fruit, nut or vegetable left in the residual bin. Layer-1 observations: 16 rows, 1827–1866. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×14; KEW_CUST 4 ×2. Units: Bushels ×16. Top routes: World → United Kingdom ×3; Other Parts → United Kingdom ×3; Canada → England ×2; Spain → United Kingdom ×2.",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n217"
        }
      ],
      "oe:firstAttested/#resource": 1827,
