    {
      "@id": "htc:14/1431/#concept",
      "@type": [
        "skos:Concept",
        "crm:E55_Type"
      ],
      "rdfs:label": {
        "@value": "Verdegris",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:inScheme": {
        "@id": "htc:scheme/#resource"
      },
      "skos:notation": "1431",
      "skos:prefLabel": {
        "@value": "Verdegris",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:broader": {
        "@id": "htc:grp/grp_natural_dyestuffs/#concept"
      },
      "oe:observationCount/#resource": 11,
      "oe:groundingStatus/#resource": "local_concept_only",
      "oe:substanceHead/#resource": "Verdegris",
      "oe:processingState/#resource": "unspecified",
      "oe:ghostAcresDomain/#resource": "drugs_botanicals_chemicals",
      "oe:attestationBasis/#resource": "customs_enumerated",
      "oe:landBasis/#resource": "terrestrial_crop",
      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "cultivated",
      "oe:theodoridisBucket/#resource": "raw_materials",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_53_Dyeing_Tanning_Colouring",
      "oe:isAggregate/#resource": false,
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(GER. *Grünspan*. DU. *Spaansch groen*. DA. *Spansk grønt*. SW. *Spank gröna*. FR. *Verde-gris*, *Verdet*. IT. *Verderame*. SP. *Cardenillo*, *Verdete*, *Verde-gris*. PORT. *Verdete*, *Verdegris*, *Cardinillo*. RUS. *Jar*. POL. *Gryszpan*. LAT. *Ærugo*, *Viride æris*,) is an acetat of copper, useful in the arts as a pigment. The principles on which it is formed are these : Acetic acid attacks copper very slowly in open vessels, converts it into an oxide, and dissolves it; but in close vessels no action takes place. This acid readily combines with the oxide of copper, and forms with it an acetat. This salt was known by the ancients; and various ways of preparing it are described by Pliny. It is usually obtained by exposing plates of copper to the action of vinegar, till they are converted to a blueish green powder, and then dissolving this powder in acetic acid, and crystallising it. Acetat of copper crystallises in four-sided truncated pyramids. [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 980]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:editorialNote": [
        {
          "@value": "Curated classification (group=grp_natural_dyestuffs): Named dye or pigment (annotto, orchella, weld, rock moss = orchil lichen per Mortimer 1823 s.v. ROCK MOSS 'See ORCHILLA', vermilion, zaffre, verdigris, lampblack) left in the residual bin. Layer-1 observations: 11 rows, 1800–1848. Series: KEW_CUST 5 ×9; KEW_CUST 4 ×2. Units: Lbs. (Pounds) ×10; Hundredweights.Quarters.Pounds ×1. Top routes: Canada → England ×2; France → United Kingdom ×1; Holland → United Kingdom ×1; Italy → United Kingdom ×1.",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n980"
        }
      ],
