      "oe:theodoridisBucket/#resource": "manufactured_articles",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_04_Cereals_and_Preparations",
      "oe:isAggregate/#resource": false,
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a chemical name for what is termed British gum, the calcined starch of wheat or potatoes. It is largely used for stiffening fabrics, and also for the adhesive substance on postage and receipt stamps, and envelopes. About fifty or sixty tons are used weekly in the print-works of Glasgow and Manchester. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 137]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:editorialNote": [
        {
          "@value": "Curated classification (group=grp_cereals_grains; processing_state=manufactured): Farinaceous preparations (macaroni, vermicelli, crackers/biscuit, starch, dextrine) belong with Cereals; other starch headings (htc:1561, htc:1572) are already here. Layer-1 observations: 5 rows, 1906–1906. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×5. Units: CWTS (Hundredweights) ×5. Top routes: World → United Kingdom ×1; United States of America → United Kingdom ×1; Germany → United Kingdom ×1; Other Foreign Countries → United Kingdom ×1.",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
