        "@value": "a stimulating gum-resin, obtained in India from *Roswellia thurifera*, used medicinally and as a perfume. *See FRANKINCENSE*. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 277]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:editorialNote": [
        {
          "@value": "Curated classification (group=grp_gums_resins_naval_stores): Named gum-resin under an archaic spelling (olibanum; 'Benjamin' = benzoin, Simmonds 1892 s.v. BENJAMIN; 'camphire' = camphor) left in the residual bin. Layer-1 observations: 3 rows, 1866–1866. Series: HCPP_Annual Statement of Trade ×3. Units: CWTS (Hundredweights) ×3. Top routes: World → United Kingdom ×1; Other Parts → United Kingdom ×1; Bombay and Scinde → United Kingdom ×1.",
          "@language": "en"
        }
