      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the *Agrimonia eupatoria*, a British plant, which has long been used to make an herb tea, and is said to have some medicinal properties: a volatile oil may be obtained from the plant, and it has been used for dyeing wool and dressing leather. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 438]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 438.",
