      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_61_Leather_and_Dressed_Furskins",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "leather made from various kinds of skins, dressed with fish-oil. The oil is hammered or beaten by a mill into the pores of the skin, which is afterwards partially dried, and washed in strong alkali, when it becomes very soft and pliable. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 94]",
