          "@value": "Gum Dragon's Blood",
          "@language": "en"
        },
        {
          "@value": "sanguis draconis",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a resinous astringent extract, of a deep red colour, obtained from the fleshy fruits of the *Calamus Draco*, a plant of Sumatra and the Malay islands. It is chiefly used as a colouring ingredient for spirit and turpentine varnishes and paints, for staining marble, preparing gold lacker, dentifrices, &c. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 144]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0220 (Gum Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 144, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 275.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
