          "@value": "Gentian Root",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Gentian root comes from the genus of flowering plants, Gentiana. (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gentiana) Gentian is used to treat a wide range of ailments in the Compendium, including gout, scrofula, worms, dyspepsia, epilepsy, and jaundice. [Danika Drugs Project: British Pharmacopoeia (1867) / Savory (1865)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Danika Drugs.xlsx (British Pharmacopoeia 1867 / Savory 1865), 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 459, J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 729.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
