      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "forest_harvest",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_24_Cork_and_Wood",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a common name for many trees, producing hard, ponderous, close-grained woods; in America, for the *Ostrya Virginica*, a tree which only grows to a small size, but the white wood is compact, finely grained and heavy. There is an iron-wood in Brazil, but the tree yielding it is not defined. Another iron-wood entering into commerce is the *Metrosideros verus*, brought from China. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 218]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0070 (Wood Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 218, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 543.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
