      "oe:landBasis/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "are excrescences produced by the puncture of an insect upon the flower-cups of several species of oak. They are compressed or flat, irregularly pointed, generally prickly and hard; brown when ripe. They abound in Styria, Croatia, Sclavonia, and Natolia; those from the latter country being the best. [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0041 (Bark For Tanners and Dyers Use). Attested in: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 478.",
