      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the *Origanum vulgare*, used as a seasoning in cookery, the powder as an ermine, the tea for nervousness. This plant also yields what is called the oil of thyme in the shops, a common remedy for tooth-ache. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 253]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0408 (Spices Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 253, Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 622.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
