      },
      "oe:groundingStatus/#resource": "document_attested_local",
      "oe:ghostAcresDomain/#resource": "drugs_botanicals_chemicals",
      "oe:landBasis/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(**GER.** *Napelgelb*, *Neapolitanisch gelb*. **DU.** *Napels geel*. **DA.** and **SW.** *Neapelgult*. **FR.** *Jaune de Naples*. **IT.** *Giallolino*. **SP.** *Amarillo de Napoles*. **PORT.** *Amarelo de Napoles*. **RUS.** *Spelt Neapolitanskaja*.) According to Professor Beckmann, this colour is prepared by calcining lead with antimony and potash in a reverboratory furnace. The proportions are different, but the antimony appears never to exceed four-fifths of the lead, or be less than half; and the potash varies from two-fifths of the weight of the metals to an eighteenth. Sometimes also common salt is added. [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 654]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs Unenumerated). Attested in: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 654, P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 269.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n654"
        },
        {
