      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Sassafras Wood",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "Sassafras is a tree native to eastern North America and eastern Asia.  Oil can be produced from the dried bark, and it leaves were rubbed into wounds by Native American tribespeople. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sassafras Sassafras is not mentioned in great length in the Pharmacopeia or the Compendium, with no description of the plant or any of its medical uses being provided. [Danika Drugs Project: British Pharmacopoeia (1867) / Savory (1865)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0122 (Drugs Unenumerated). Attested in: Danika Drugs.xlsx (British Pharmacopoeia 1867 / Savory 1865), 1866 British Customs Commodities List (Danika / HGIS Lab), P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 502. Research note: smilax?.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n502"
        }
      ],
