      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "the *Dipsacus fullonum*, a plant cultivated to a considerable extent in the woolen cloth manufacturing districts, for its use in raising the nap upon those stuffs, which it does by means of the rigid hooked awns or chaff of the heads. upwards of twenty millions of these teasel heads are also imported annually from France. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 387]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0822 (Esparto and other Vegetable Fibres for making Paper). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 387, Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562).",
