        "@id": "htc:00/0054/#concept"
      },
      "oe:groundingStatus/#resource": "document_attested_local",
      "oe:ghostAcresDomain/#resource": "drugs_botanicals_chemicals",
      "oe:landBasis/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(GER. *Bergblau*, *Kupferblau*. DU. *Bergblaauw*. DA. *Biergblaat*. SW. *Bergblätt*. FR. *Cendres bleues*. IT. *Azzurro di montagna*. SP. *Verde-Tierra*. PORT. *Verdemontanha azul*. RUS. *Golubez*, *Medndia lazur*. LAT. *Cæruleum montanum*,) a kind of mineral substance, used by painters, &c for a blue; but more usually mixed with a yellow for a green colour. Verditer, according to M. Savary, ought to be made of the *lapis armenus*; or at least of an earthy substance much like it, brought from the mountains of Hungary, &c. only prepared by powdering it, and cleansing it by lotion. [Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 980]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs Unenumerated). Attested in: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 980, P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 411.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
        {
          "@id": "https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n980"
