      "oe:landBasis/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "unclassified",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "oe:sitcUnassigned",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a native name in British Guiana, for the monkey-pot tree, *Lecythis grandiflora*, Aublet. It grows to a large size. The wood is hard, close-grained, and handsome; it is used for furniture, and makes good staves for hogsheads. [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 415]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0070 (Wood Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 415.",
