      "oe:harvestMode/#resource": "cultivated",
      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_53_Dyeing_Tanning_Colouring",
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "(_Vouëde_, _Pastel_, Fr.; _Waid_, Germ; _Isatis tinctoria_, Linn.); the _glastum_ of the antient Gauls and Germans; is an herbaceous plant which was formerly much cultivated, as affording a permanent blue dye, but it has been in modern times well nigh superseded by _indigo_. [Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562)]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0054 (Dye Stuffs Unenumerated). Attested in: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (Project Gutenberg #44562), Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 1018.",
