      "oe:sitcCode/#resource": "SITC4_05_Vegetables_and_Fruit",
      "skos:altLabel": [
        {
          "@value": "Affoo Yam",
          "@language": "en"
        }
      ],
      "skos:definition": {
        "@value": "a climbing plant, with large tuberous roots, forming one of the bread-stuffs of the W. Indies, and other tropical regions, of which there are several species. The roots are very rich in nutritive fecula; hence they are much used as articles of diet. The common yam is the *Dioscorea sativa*; the wing-stalked or red-yam (*D. alata*); the prickly-yam (*D. aculeata*); [P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 430]",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": {
        "@value": "Document-attested minor 19th-century commodity absent as a standalone line in Layer-1 British/Canadian customs ledgers; typically subsumed under htc:0085 (Vegetables Unenumerated). Attested in: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 430.",
        "@language": "en"
      },
      "oe:dictionarySource/#resource": [
